#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221191 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4874a486-2cf1-5b6e-ae4f-52579f03b077" :question/math-academy-id "e-21695" :question/problem "![](images/28/28eeada9d539377f372fd73155f08215e6a0eb3b3503b88e05c85e38977c024a.svg)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system." :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x},$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n![](images/89/892921f10f5b3db34b140a6d7608bdb4771fd1ad055319e7ff5d4edceb527d3a.svg)\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221192 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/id #uuid "3e053d6d-f051-5c4c-a20b-1e90b2d5941b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21696" :question/problem "![](images/23/23c904878889e799744f2f4df095727d82a26050d15a01e0df06be6e3838b48d.svg)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/74/744b1de9acba2740bac7ebc54d79fb7ae82e98cb0576492f4d9503bb0b2a71a8.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221193 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Properties of a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "a52a6539-60d1-557c-a042-cbce5616e94f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21697" :question/problem "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[ \\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$*\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) =  \\big[ A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that ![](images/03/036695dd1a6ae9a121d25cae37b1ba7be7e77502b99335391da8d1d8d04972be.svg).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](images/27/27f914bbca34dfde3debdcf58eb5a2205eda059ceafa10bf6964bb7c6a984d1c.svg).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line ![](images/b4/b48e9d724fed6566873ba6c844fcfa10c92fcd810012da503960972f8c4105c3.svg)." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}.$ Since $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ is a solution of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n\\Phi'(t)=\\big[A\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:A\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]\n= A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by factoring out $A$ on the left.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}.$ From $\\textrm{L6}$ we have $\\Phi'(t)=A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].$ Using $\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$ from $\\textrm{L1},$ we substitute to obtain $\\Phi'(t)=A\\Phi(t).$ Thus, $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from $\\textrm{L6}$ by substitution from $L1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0388fd29-b8b2-576b-a6c9-9265590bee1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442485} {:answer/id #uuid "135f6c78-b365-50be-89db-444d4d16ab70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442489} {:answer/id #uuid "3c74cfd1-a038-5c50-bfb1-5cecb7ed1534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-2} & -{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{-2} & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442501} {:answer/id #uuid "bb791218-a743-55a3-afe5-6608ffd45328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442524} {:answer/id #uuid "feb3ec21-6632-5781-b732-b19423bbbfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb3ec21-6632-5781-b732-b19423bbbfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442536} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bff80bf-991a-5e44-9565-955b85bdc117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f190e3a4-5efc-5caa-a01f-c8d68875b312" :question/math-academy-id "q-333701" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-2t} & 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b62f64-a72a-58c3-83b7-f04a2c64a243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305212} {:answer/id #uuid "1727af84-c111-5aa6-af98-970d01d617df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1727af84-c111-5aa6-af98-970d01d617df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442492} :answer-field/id #uuid "38ab63d4-df22-5b52-9665-41a9dd7da8dd" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29616b45-1873-5c52-bde7-ea7fad263666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305540} {:answer/id #uuid "18f3666c-fa87-56a3-a25f-71b0d5b23487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f3666c-fa87-56a3-a25f-71b0d5b23487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442493} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f8022a-afa7-5402-9578-ce5abfc3468c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307701} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d6721b-ff98-5c3e-ad88-04c1ddec1e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307378} {:answer/id #uuid "caa9d9cb-c73f-5b34-8a78-86a7733240c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa9d9cb-c73f-5b34-8a78-86a7733240c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442528} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c6472ca-2c4e-5da6-9f5a-4788af95a430" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94eeb383-7b17-545d-8da7-02f82d01ed4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306553} {:answer/id #uuid "62e44a13-82dc-5b4c-af8f-b45690546571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e44a13-82dc-5b4c-af8f-b45690546571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442511} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2155b73-1362-5f64-bf35-7930dcaeeb4a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca7cf8a-29b9-5dc9-9eb7-2eb98441be57" :question/math-academy-id "q-333713" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Properties of a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e315641f-7048-5d22-999e-3be3cfc5af5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391266} {:answer/id #uuid "0233e06e-a302-51e4-b083-fe0aaa4725aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391267} {:answer/id #uuid "be3009e6-e7cf-57fb-8c1f-a885361311eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391268} {:answer/id #uuid "cf515a09-9dba-5d02-ae42-355ff706ce78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e315641f-7048-5d22-999e-3be3cfc5af5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391266} :answer-field/id #uuid "85305209-f127-50f4-8fb6-558c6f7e40e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a700d19b-464e-5807-9333-12ea7e2f2e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391271} {:answer/id #uuid "9667e6c0-3577-5729-b0b1-c1fd903f6823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391272} {:answer/id #uuid "c43f1625-204a-5fc4-acbc-a60997cdf542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391273} {:answer/id #uuid "27100933-11fb-5602-87aa-f18a23a2da08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9667e6c0-3577-5729-b0b1-c1fd903f6823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391272} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a119ab6-43f8-5872-ab0c-26e93853cfdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391270} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d630e396-6325-5e09-89df-19e69a52dddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391276} {:answer/id #uuid "63c0991b-ad6e-5f6a-98b8-643da1aae492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transposing the matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391277} {:answer/id #uuid "351118ef-d9b7-5b2c-8103-6248dbbc0401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391278} {:answer/id #uuid "cafa980d-badb-57fc-a8c3-a2ffc2a64bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ(t)$ to both sides" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d630e396-6325-5e09-89df-19e69a52dddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391276} :answer-field/id #uuid "155738af-3e2a-57a9-a635-a3db6456eb1e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49e5968a-1ea4-5510-81b8-9a383e60e6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333719" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L4}$ and substitution from lines {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{1}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=A[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=AΦ(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Properties of a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8335968c-9d4c-5fbb-9ffa-75dcaa53d606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b01ba2-8f40-5f19-b36b-a5bc0a007ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220593} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f3e3a-b149-5084-b607-3cd5fd0d18db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220594} {:answer/id #uuid "6493f73f-365f-5404-a5da-5e40752fbe4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8335968c-9d4c-5fbb-9ffa-75dcaa53d606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592} :answer-field/id #uuid "93036466-1c3c-5e59-947c-e8ff8d157797" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220591} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e243901-1d01-5ec9-9cdc-6cb103946683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442487} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcb761f-eaed-55a9-ad0a-3da04843c7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442494} {:answer/id #uuid "4832e2c7-75c3-5827-8346-bf8d89ce6137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442505} {:answer/id #uuid "f947d07b-6d00-57b7-a3fe-19a08102e66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dcb761f-eaed-55a9-ad0a-3da04843c7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442494} :answer-field/id #uuid "dccda4ab-5506-5030-89e0-e232b37f3a8b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967d6780-0d69-5d91-b012-b91bfef745e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220602} {:answer/id #uuid "e753d893-e42e-50d3-bb33-dac9396f5d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the zero factor theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220604} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2aa18-1cb1-5685-99ca-77c79bd3efdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of determinants" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220605} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6b0878-88fc-5098-98f5-9eeb896f07d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2aa18-1cb1-5685-99ca-77c79bd3efdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of determinants" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220605} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb71a74-1627-56af-a5ab-668a47e38b61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "920ced52-32ff-52a3-a836-fb31a23c82e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333722" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the properties of determinants}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a6f6fa-d163-59c9-8941-abbf24e3752e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442491} {:answer/id #uuid "23e9ec62-9362-5bd5-ab4e-ea2a807a4d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442496} {:answer/id #uuid "5416c979-f7bd-5995-af0d-eb24dc7ca789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2} & -{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2} & 3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442507} {:answer/id #uuid "83d316e3-9443-5fad-b4ad-57ed67049c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442515} {:answer/id #uuid "ce05c0b9-0887-59c7-99c8-8f8b04b83fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e9ec62-9362-5bd5-ab4e-ea2a807a4d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442496} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a95112-8d67-5abc-bdfb-cee7755e29a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c4c6b4c-9f70-5e9c-b320-c7133b929a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-334275" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental matrix could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b36e15d-837a-5f85-9a7f-91f066b4c4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442498} {:answer/id #uuid "434d7ea8-e63a-51b4-84d2-ca9a28788176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442502} {:answer/id #uuid "7038d0fd-b4be-5ac4-85f1-ceeccda1e156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4} & {e}^{4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442513} {:answer/id #uuid "a8abf479-af90-51de-b6dd-86215d035d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442521} {:answer/id #uuid "bddbb522-2d10-5aec-9ed3-ceed476b3b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8abf479-af90-51de-b6dd-86215d035d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442521} :answer-field/id #uuid "509a43d8-3e68-51c3-84c7-64c7f6910514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f204e80-4d56-5e54-8e44-fafc75ad1af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334325" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-poin